# ==============================================================================
# MaizLink -- the configuration template for the Docker stack
# ==============================================================================
#
#   cp .env.docker.example .env.docker
#   flask security generate-secrets --write .env.docker --with-service-passwords
#   docker compose --env-file .env.docker up -d --build
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this file is, and why there are two of them
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same catalogue as `.env.example`, with eight lines set for a stack that
# runs entirely in containers. Every other line is identical, and a test
# holds that true: `test_the_two_templates_do_not_drift.py` fails when one
# template grows a key the other lacks, or when a value differs outside the
# eight named below.
#
# There are two files because one cannot answer two questions at once. The
# single template used to say `COMPOSE_PROFILES=db,cache,broker` -- bring up
# PostgreSQL and two Redis -- and `DATABASE_TYPE=sqlite` -- and work with
# SQLite -- on the same page. Following it produced a stack whose containers
# were healthy and whose application went past all of them: the migration
# wrote a schema into a throwaway container, the application opened an empty
# file beside it, `/health` answered `healthy` and the landing page answered
# `500 no such table: roles`. Measured on a fresh clone.
#
# The nine settings that differ from `.env.example`. Written with arrows
# rather than as assignments on purpose: a line of the form NAME=value in
# this header would be read as a declaration by anything that parses the
# file, this project's own drift test included.
#
#   ENV_FILE       -> .env.docker    compose hands this file to the
#                                    containers, so it has to name itself
#   DATABASE_TYPE  -> postgresql     the db profile brings one up; SQLite
#                                    here is the failure described above
#   DATABASE_HOST  -> db             the service name inside the network
#   DATABASE_NAME  -> db_shortener   a database name, not a file name
#   DATABASE_USER  -> shortener      what the db service creates
#   REDIS_ENABLED  -> true           the cache profile brings one up
#   CELERY_ENABLED -> true           the broker profile brings one up
#   DOMAIN         -> localhost:5000 the name short links are built from.
#                                    Change it to your own host before
#                                    anyone else uses this
#   LOG_TO_FILE    -> true           this file ships `logs` in
#                                    COMPOSE_PROFILES, which brings up the
#                                    rotator; without the files it rotates
#                                    that container runs for nobody and
#                                    every journal page reads empty
#
# Two values are deliberately left empty and are filled by the command
# above: DATABASE_PASSWORD and REDIS_PASSWORD. The repository ships no
# default password, and the services refuse to start without one rather than
# come up open -- see the `db` and `redis` services in
# dockers/docker-compose.yml.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running something outside the stack
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Take its profile out of COMPOSE_PROFILES and name the address instead:
#
#   drop `db`      -> set DATABASE_URL
#   drop `cache`   -> set REDIS_URL
#   drop `broker`  -> set CELERY_BROKER_URL
#   drop `mail`    -> set MAIL_HOST and MAIL_PORT
#
# The application is reached the same way from outside: the db, cache and
# broker publish their ports on the loopback, so `flask run` on the host can
# use the containers while the application itself stays local. The matrix of
# combinations, with the values for each, is in docs/getting-started.md.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How to read this file
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every variable has one line of description, in the form:
#
#   # what it does · accepted values · where it applies
#
# "Where it applies" is the profile chosen through FLASK_ENV:
#
#   dev      development -- local development
#   stag     staging     -- pre-production
#   prod     production  -- live service
#   compose  the variable is read by docker compose, not by the application
#
# The testing profile never appears in these lists: it reads neither .env nor
# real environment variables -- the automated tests have to give the same
# answer on any machine, and take their values from TestingConfig alone.
#
# Precedence of sources, the topmost wins:
#
#   1. a real environment variable (export VAR=..., environment: in compose)
#   2. .env.<profile>              for example .env.production
#   3. .env                        this file
#   4. the profile's default       code in infrastructure/configs/app/
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The minimum without which staging and production WILL NOT START
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# With an empty environment these profiles refuse to start and name five
# reasons. development and testing come up without a single variable set.
#
#   SECRET_KEY           signs JWTs and sessions; the default is generated at
#                        random per process, so every worker has a different
#                        one and no worker accepts another's tokens
#   SHORT_CODE_PEPPER    the salt code generation uses; let it differ between
#                        instances and the codes for one URL differ too
#   DOMAIN               the address every short link is built from; without
#                        it HOST:PORT is used -- the address the process
#                        listens on rather than the one people arrive at
#   DATABASE_TYPE=postgresql
#                        plus DATABASE_HOST, DATABASE_NAME, DATABASE_USER --
#                        or all of it as one string in DATABASE_URL. These
#                        profiles do not start on SQLite: the defaults would
#                        create an empty new file, and the service would
#                        answer as though the data had never existed.
#                        DATABASE_PASSWORD is not required -- peer, trust and
#                        .pgpass work without one
#   REDIS_URL            on these profiles REDIS_ENABLED=true by default, and
#                        Redis turned on with no address is a cache and a rate
#                        limiter with nothing to connect to
#
# Conditionally required: MAIL_HOST and MAIL_FROM -- if MAIL_ENABLED=true.
# ==============================================================================


# ==============================================================================
# 1. Profile and start-up
# ==============================================================================

# Which configuration profile to use · development | staging | production |
# testing · everywhere. Unset means development
FLASK_ENV=development

# The entry point for the flask command lives in `.flaskenv`, not here.
# Flask reads that file on every run, so the project's command groups exist
# before any env file does -- which is what the Docker path needs, since it
# never creates this file at all. Setting FLASK_APP here as well would work
# and would be a second place to keep it right.

# Debugger and autoreload of the built-in flask run server · 1 | 0 · dev.
# It does not affect the profile itself: DEBUG is set by the profile and no
# variable changes it
FLASK_DEBUG=1


# ==============================================================================
# 2. Secrets                                             REQUIRED on stag/prod
# ==============================================================================
# Both are generated by `flask security generate-secrets`.
# Never commit them filled in.

# Signs JWTs, sessions and cache entries · a 64-byte hex string · everywhere.
# REQUIRED on stag/prod: the default is random per process, so tokens die on
# a restart and do not carry between workers
SECRET_KEY=

# The salt for hashing a URL when a code is generated · any long string ·
# everywhere. REQUIRED on stag/prod: different values across instances give
# different codes for one and the same address
SHORT_CODE_PEPPER=


# ==============================================================================
# 3. The address of the service
# ==============================================================================

# The interface the server listens on · an IP or a host name · everywhere.
# 127.0.0.1 -- this machine only, 0.0.0.0 -- every interface.
# Without DOMAIN the address in a short link is built from this too, so
# 0.0.0.0 produces links like http://0.0.0.0:5000/<code>.
# The profile's default is localhost; in a container the binding is set by
# ${HOST:-0.0.0.0} from the image's CMD
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# The port the server listens on · 1..65535 · everywhere.
# Default: 5000, and 8000 in prod
PORT=5000

# Where a local `flask run` binds · substituted from HOST and PORT · dev.
# The Flask CLI knows only these two names, so what needs changing is HOST
# and PORT rather than this pair.
# Both lines must come AFTER the ones they refer to: substitution in an env
# file is resolved as the file is read. ${HOST} needs a default because HOST
# itself is commented out.
FLASK_RUN_HOST=${HOST:-localhost}
FLASK_RUN_PORT=${PORT}

# The public host name every short link is built from · a host name without
# scheme or path, a port allowed (short.example.com:8443) · everywhere.
# REQUIRED on stag/prod. A value with a scheme (https://short...) would give
# https://https://short... and is refused at startup; the shape of the name
# beyond that is DNS's business, not the application's
DOMAIN=localhost:5000

# Build public links as https · true | false · everywhere.
# The profile knows: false on dev, true on stag/prod. Set it explicitly only
# if the deployment runs without TLS
# USE_HTTPS=false

# How long a browser remembers to reach this service over TLS only, in
# seconds · 0..∞ · read only where USE_HTTPS is on, so a plain-HTTP run never
# sends the header. A year, because the request it protects is the first one
# of a later visit -- the one still made over http:// and still answerable by
# whoever is on the path. 0 turns it off, which is the setting when the
# reverse proxy in front sends the header itself: two of them are not
# additive and the browser reads the first. Deliberately without
# includeSubDomains and preload -- both speak for more than this service
# HSTS_MAX_AGE=31536000

# The port the application is published on the host under · 1..65535 · compose
APP_HOST_PORT=5000


# ==============================================================================
# 4. The database
# ==============================================================================

# --- Which database ---

# The kind of database · sqlite | postgresql · everywhere.
# Only postgresql is allowed on stag/prod -- SQLite there would mean an empty
# new file in place of the data
DATABASE_TYPE=postgresql

# The database name, or for sqlite the file name · a string · everywhere.
# A relative name lands in the directory from DATABASE_DIR rather than the
# process's working directory: otherwise `flask` started from src/ would open
# a second, empty file and say nothing about it. An absolute path is taken as
# it stands and DATABASE_DIR is then not read
DATABASE_NAME=db_shortener

# The directory holding the sqlite files · a path · everywhere. Postgres does
# not read it. A relative one is counted from the project root; an absolute
# one is taken as it stands and works in a container too, where there is no
# project root (the package sits in site-packages) -- that is how a mounted
# volume is named, /var/lib/app for instance. The directory creates itself
DATABASE_DIR=datas/databases

# A ready-made connection string, whole · a SQLAlchemy URL · everywhere.
# When it is set, none of the DATABASE_* above or below is read at all.
# Example: postgresql+psycopg://user:pass@localhost:5432/db_shortener
# DATABASE_URL=

# --- Connection (postgresql only) ---

# The address of the database server · a host name or IP · everywhere. In a
# docker network this is the service name
DATABASE_HOST=db

# The port of the database server · 1..65535 · everywhere.
# In docker it also sets the port the PostgreSQL container listens on: one
# value instead of the image's port and the application's setting
DATABASE_PORT=5432

# The database user · a string · everywhere. REQUIRED on stag/prod when there
# is no DATABASE_URL
DATABASE_USER=shortener

# The user's password · a string · everywhere. Not required: peer, trust,
# .pgpass and PGPASSWORD all work without one
DATABASE_PASSWORD=

# --- Timeouts and pool (postgresql only) ---

# How long to wait for a connection, in seconds · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere. Without it we wait out the OS's TCP timeout -- minutes -- while
# the healthcheck gives up after 10 seconds
DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=3

# The ceiling on one SQL statement, in seconds · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere. A server that has frozen while its kernel still answers is not
# closed out by anything else
DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=10

# Persistent connections in the pool · a whole number > 0 · everywhere.
# SQLAlchemy reads zero as "no limit" and silently cancels
# DATABASE_MAX_OVERFLOW along with it -- keep the value positive.
# It is per process: a sync worker runs one request and holds one connection.
# Choosing a value -- docs/development.md, "Load profile"
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=5

# Extra connections above the pool at peak · a whole number, -1 = no limit ·
# everywhere. The ceiling is multiplied by the number of workers, and
# PostgreSQL allows a hundred connections by default
DATABASE_MAX_OVERFLOW=5

# Recreate a connection after N seconds · a whole number, -1 = never ·
# everywhere. Protection against the network dropping idle connections
DATABASE_POOL_RECYCLE=3600

# Test a connection with a query before handing it out of the pool ·
# true | false · everywhere. It costs one extra round trip and survives a
# restart of the database
DATABASE_POOL_PRE_PING=true

# --- Schema and data ---

# Print every SQL statement to the log · true | false · dev.
# Leave it false on stag/prod: field values end up in the logs
SQLALCHEMY_ECHO=false

# Manage the schema through alembic · true | false · everywhere.
# false opens up `flask db init` and `flask db drop`, which create and drop
# tables directly, going around the revisions
USE_ALEMBIC=true

# A connection string for a single alembic command · a SQLAlchemy URL ·
# everywhere. It overrides everything else and needs no working application
# configuration -- for migrating a database the service is not yet set up for
# ALEMBIC_DATABASE_URL=

# Create the system roles again at every start · true | false · everywhere.
# The profile knows: true on dev, false on stag/prod, where the roles are
# seeded once by `flask db load-base-roles`
# AUTO_SEED_ROLES=true

# --- Publishing to the host (docker) ---

# The PostgreSQL port on the host · 1..65535 · compose
DATABASE_HOST_PORT=5432

# The address the database port is published on the host under · an IP ·
# compose. The loopback means "visible from this machine only"; 0.0.0.0 hands
# the database to the whole network, with only the password defending it
DATABASE_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1


# ==============================================================================
# 5. Cache and Redis
# ==============================================================================
# Three working combinations: no cache (CACHE_ENABLED=false), a cache in the
# process's memory (CACHE_ENABLED=true, REDIS_ENABLED=false), a cache in
# Redis (both true).

# Use a cache at all · true | false · everywhere.
# false -- every call into the cache becomes a no-op
CACHE_ENABLED=true

# Whether the deployment has Redis · true | false · everywhere.
# The profile knows: false on dev, true on stag/prod. It governs more than
# the cache: the rate limiter asks this variable too
REDIS_ENABLED=true

# The Redis address · redis://[:password@]host:port/db-number · everywhere.
# REQUIRED on stag/prod when REDIS_ENABLED=true.
# In docker, empty means the Redis from this same stack; a value set here
# overrides compose's substitution -- that is how an external or managed one
# is connected
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# The Redis password compose builds REDIS_URL from · a string · compose
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# The port Redis listens on inside the docker network · 1..65535 ·
# everywhere. REDIS_URL for the stack's containers is built from it too, so
# changing it here is enough. For an external Redis the port goes straight
# into REDIS_URL
REDIS_PORT=6379

# How long to wait for a Redis connection, in seconds · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere
REDIS_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=2

# How long to wait for an answer to a command, in seconds · a whole number
# > 0 · everywhere
REDIS_SOCKET_TIMEOUT=2

# The pause before trying again after a disconnection, in seconds · a whole
# number > 0 · everywhere. While the pause lasts the cache answers with a
# miss rather than waiting for a connection
REDIS_RETRY_INTERVAL=10

# The prefix this service's keys carry in Redis · a string · everywhere.
# Different prefixes let several installations share one server
CACHE_LINK_PREFIX=link_shortener

# How long a link's cache entry lives, in seconds · a whole number >= 0 ·
# everywhere. Default: 3600, and 20 on dev so an edit shows up at once
CACHE_LINK_TTL=3600

# How long the cached summary statistics live, in seconds · a whole number
# >= 0 · everywhere. Default: 300, and 20 on dev
CACHE_STATS_TTL=300

# The Redis port on the host · 1..65535 · compose
REDIS_HOST_PORT=6379


# ==============================================================================
# 6. Background work (Celery)
# ==============================================================================
# Celery turned off loses no work: counting clicks and sending mail are done
# synchronously, inside the request handler itself.

# Hand work to a background worker · true | false · everywhere
CELERY_ENABLED=true

# The address of the task broker · redis://[:password@]host:port/db-number ·
# everywhere. Needed when CELERY_ENABLED=true. A different Redis database
# from the cache: clearing the cache must not wipe the queue. In docker,
# empty means the broker from this stack, a value set here means any
# external one
CELERY_BROKER_URL=

# Where to put task results · the same format · everywhere.
# Empty -- results are not kept; the tasks here have no use for them
CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND=

# How long to wait for the broker, in seconds · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere. Without it queueing a task waits for the broker forever,
# holding the request
CELERY_BROKER_TIMEOUT=2

# The Redis broker's port on the host · 1..65535 · compose.
# Not 6380: that port is taken by the test stack's Redis
REDIS_BROKER_HOST_PORT=6381

# The port the broker listens on inside the docker network · 1..65535 ·
# compose. Separate from REDIS_PORT: the cache and the queue are different
# servers, and keeping them on different ports is sometimes needed when both
# are published outward
REDIS_BROKER_PORT=6379


# ==============================================================================
# 7. Mail and address confirmation
# ==============================================================================
# With mail turned off registration goes through, but there is nothing to
# confirm the address with -- such an account cannot be signed in to.

# Send mail · true | false · everywhere.
# With true, MAIL_HOST and MAIL_FROM are REQUIRED -- otherwise start-up is
# aborted. Without mail registration goes through and there is nothing to
# confirm the address with
MAIL_ENABLED=true

# The sending server · a host name · everywhere. The default on dev is
# localhost (the Mailpit catcher); on stag/prod there is no default.
# In docker, empty means the catcher from this same stack
MAIL_HOST=

# The sending port · 587 STARTTLS | 465 SSL | 1025 Mailpit · everywhere.
# The profile knows: 1025 on dev, 587 on stag/prod
# MAIL_PORT=587

# The account on the sending server · a string · everywhere.
# Empty -- an unauthenticated connection, as with the local catcher
MAIL_USERNAME=

# Its password · a string · everywhere.
# Set without MAIL_USERNAME, start-up is aborted: there is nothing to
# authenticate with
MAIL_PASSWORD=

# Encrypt the connection with STARTTLS · true | false · everywhere.
# Mutually exclusive with MAIL_USE_SSL.
# The profile knows: false on dev, true on stag/prod, where starting without
# encryption is aborted
# MAIL_USE_TLS=true

# Connect over TLS from the start, port 465 · true | false · everywhere
MAIL_USE_SSL=false

# The address in the From field · a mail address · everywhere.
# REQUIRED when MAIL_ENABLED=true. Empty means the profile's default:
# no-reply@link-shortener.local on dev; on stag/prod there is no default
MAIL_FROM=

# How long to wait for the mail server, in seconds · a number > 0 ·
# everywhere
MAIL_TIMEOUT=10

# How long a confirmation link lives, in hours · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere
EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TTL_HOURS=24

# How long a password-reset link lives, in minutes · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere. Minutes rather than hours: a confirmation proves the mailbox
# is read, while this link opens the account itself and sits in the mailbox
# for all of that time
PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_MINUTES=60

# After how long an unconfirmed account is deleted, in hours · a whole
# number > 0 · everywhere. It cannot be smaller than
# EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TTL_HOURS: otherwise the account is removed before the
# link sent to it has expired
UNVERIFIED_ACCOUNT_TTL_HOURS=72

# The local mail catcher's ports on the host · 1..65535 · compose.
# SMTP accepts the mail, the UI shows it in a browser
MAILPIT_SMTP_PORT=1025
MAILPIT_UI_PORT=8025


# ==============================================================================
# 8. Logs, audit and observability
# ==============================================================================

# The whole time budget for polling dependencies for /health, in seconds ·
# a number > 0 · everywhere. It is shared between all the checks rather than
# given to each: a hung database must not hold the answer longer than this
HEALTH_CHECK_TIMEOUT=5

# Keep the application journal · true | false · everywhere.
# false is the same as LOGGER_TYPE=null
LOGGING_ENABLED=true

# Keep the audit journal -- who created, opened, deleted a link ·
# true | false · everywhere. false is the same as AUDIT_TYPE=null
AUDIT_ENABLED=true

# What writes the application journal · auto | structlog | standard | null ·
# everywhere. auto and structlog give the chain structlog -> standard: if the
# first stops accepting records the work moves to the second, and returns
# when the first comes back
LOGGER_TYPE=auto

# What writes the audit journal · auto | structlog | standard | null ·
# everywhere
AUDIT_TYPE=auto

# The lowest level recorded · DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL ·
# everywhere. Default: DEBUG on dev, INFO on stag/prod
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Write to standard output · true | false · everywhere.
# The profile knows: true on dev, false on stag/prod
# LOG_TO_CONSOLE=true

# Write to files · true | false · everywhere.
# The profile knows: false on dev, true on stag/prod
#
# Set here rather than left to the profile, because this file ships
# `logs` in COMPOSE_PROFILES: that profile brings up the rotator, and a
# rotator with nothing to rotate is a container running for nobody --
# the same "profile on, switch off" pair the arrangement table forbids.
# Measured on a live walk of row 1 as shipped: the rotator came up and
# logged "logrotate will follow: application.log, error.log, audit.log",
# `datas/logs` held only its own state file, and all three journal pages
# were empty for every role entitled to read them.
LOG_TO_FILE=true

# The directory of the journal files · a path · everywhere.
# A relative one is counted from the project root, so a command started
# outside the root finds the same directory
LOG_DIR=datas/logs

# The file names inside LOG_DIR, without an extension · a string · everywhere
LOG_FILENAME=application
AUDIT_LOG_FILENAME=audit
ERROR_LOG_FILENAME=error

# The levels of other people's logs -- SQLAlchemy's and the built-in
# server's · DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL · everywhere.
# Below WARNING both are noticeably noisy
SQLALCHEMY_LOG_LEVEL=WARNING
WERKZEUG_LOG_LEVEL=WARNING

# How often to check whether the main journal has come back, in seconds ·
# a number > 0 · everywhere
FAILOVER_CHECK_INTERVAL=30


# ==============================================================================
# 9. The rules links are handled by
# ==============================================================================

# The schemes accepted for shortening · a comma-separated list · everywhere.
# There is nothing to widen it with: everything but http and https is
# refused by validation
ALLOWED_SCHEMES=http,https

# The greatest length of a target address, in characters · a whole number
# 1..2048 · everywhere. It does not go above 2048: that is what the column
# in the database allows
MAX_URL_LENGTH=2048

# Allow links into your own network · true | false · everywhere.
# Turn it on only for an installation nothing outside can reach: otherwise
# the service forwards requests from a visitor's browser into your network
ALLOW_INTERNAL_TARGETS=false

# The length of a generated code, in characters · a whole number within the
# bounds below · everywhere. A longer code collides less often, and makes a
# longer link
SHORT_CODE_LENGTH=7

# The shortest code that is accepted and may be issued · a whole number >= 6
# · everywhere. It does not go below six: that is what code validation
# requires, and the generator would be issuing what the service then refuses
SHORT_CODE_MIN_LENGTH=6

# The longest code · a whole number <= 10 · everywhere. It does not go above
# ten: that is what the urls.short_code column allows
SHORT_CODE_MAX_LENGTH=10

# How many times to try another code on a collision · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere
MAX_COLLISION_ATTEMPTS=5

# How many addresses one batch request accepts · a whole number 1..100 ·
# everywhere. It does not go above 100: a longer list is refused by the
# request schema
BATCH_CREATE_LIMIT=100

# How many links a guest may create per window · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere
GUEST_LINK_LIMIT=10

# The length of that window, in days · a whole number > 0 · everywhere
GUEST_LINK_WINDOW_DAYS=1

# How long a guest's link lives, in seconds · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere. The default is seven days. Zero here would mean "never
# expires"
DEFAULT_GUEST_TTL_SECONDS=604800

# The longest lifetime that may be asked for, in seconds · a whole number
# > 0 · everywhere. The default is ten years
MAX_TTL_SECONDS=315360000

# From how many redirects a link counts as popular · a whole number >= 0 ·
# everywhere
POPULAR_THRESHOLD=100

# Over how many recent days a link counts as fresh · a whole number >= 0 ·
# everywhere
RECENT_DAYS=7

# Whether to record every redirect as a row of its own · true | false ·
# everywhere. Off: the urls.clicks counter still grows, but the charts with
# time along the axis stay empty -- that is how the service worked before
# the visits table existed. On: one row per redirect, written by the same
# background task that moves the counter
VISIT_TRACKING_ENABLED=true

# How many days a raw visit row is kept · a whole number >= 0, 0 -- never
# delete · everywhere. A finished day is first folded into one row per link
# per day, so the daily chart survives the deletion; the breakdowns by
# device, browser and link are not kept by the roll-up -- that is exactly
# how far back they reach. It is `flask maintenance roll-up-visits` that
# deletes, not the application itself
VISIT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# How many days a raw security-event row is kept · a whole number >= 0,
# 0 -- neither fold nor delete · everywhere. Built like the visit row above
# and kept longer than it: a visit is traffic, while a sign-in, a refusal
# and a role change are evidence, and the question about one usually comes
# much later. A finished day is folded into one row per kind of event per
# day. It is `flask maintenance roll-up-security-events` that deletes, not
# the application itself
SECURITY_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=365


# ==============================================================================
# 10. Access: authentication, roles, limits, cookies
# ==============================================================================

# --- Tokens ---

# How long an access token lives, in minutes · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere
JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=15

# How long a refresh token lives, in days · a whole number > 0 · everywhere
JWT_REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=7

# The signing algorithm · HS256 | HS384 | HS512 · everywhere
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256

# --- Roles ---

# The role given at registration · the name of an existing role · everywhere.
# The role has to be seeded: `flask db load-base-roles`
DEFAULT_ROLE_NAME=user

# --- Rate limiting ---
# The limits of individual endpoints are set in code (BaseConfig.RATE_LIMITS)
# and listed in docs/OPERATIONS_AND_MIGRATIONS.md.

# The limit for every other route, requests per window · a whole number > 0 ·
# everywhere
DEFAULT_RATE_LIMIT=100

# The length of the window, in seconds · a whole number > 0 · everywhere
DEFAULT_RATE_LIMIT_PERIOD=60

# Lift the limits from sign-in and registration · true | false · dev.
# Needed so development does not lock itself out while trying passwords.
# On stag/prod this removes the defence against guessing -- leave it false
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_DISABLED=false

# --- Browser access ---

# The origins allowed to make requests with credentials · a comma-separated
# list · everywhere. A star is not allowed together with cookies
#
# Both spellings of the loopback address, and that is not belt and braces.
# The browser sends the Origin that was typed into the address bar, and the
# CSRF layer compares it with this list before letting an unsafe request from
# a cookie session through. With `localhost` alone, someone who opened
# `http://127.0.0.1:5000` sees a working front page (an anonymous caller does
# not go through CSRF) and "CSRF token missing or invalid" on every form the
# moment they sign in -- measured on a live run.
#
# Behind a real domain this line is the one to change. `staging` and
# `production` default it to empty for a reason -- an allowance for
# localhost, with credentials, is one a deployment has no use for -- but a
# value written here outranks the profile, so the default never reaches a
# deployment that copied this file. Replace both entries with your own
# origin, or empty the line: the CSRF layer admits `BASE_URL` on its own,
# so the service's own pages keep working with nothing named here.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5000,http://127.0.0.1:5000

# The proxies to be believed about X-Forwarded-For · a comma-separated list
# of IPs · everywhere. Empty -- the header is not read at all, and the client
# address is whoever connected. Name only your own balancer here: otherwise a
# client names any address it likes and walks around the guest quota
TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Send the authentication cookie over HTTPS only · true | false · everywhere.
# The profile knows: false on dev, true on stag/prod
# COOKIE_SECURE=false

# The same for Flask's own session cookie · true | false · everywhere.
# The profile knows: false on dev, true on stag/prod
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false

# When the browser attaches the session cookie to cross-site requests ·
# Strict | Lax | None · everywhere
SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE=Lax

# Hide the session cookie from JavaScript · true | false · everywhere.
# Leave it true
SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY=true


# ==============================================================================
# 11. The language of the interface
# ==============================================================================

# The languages the interface is offered in, best first · a comma-separated
# list · everywhere. The order is not decoration: Accept-Language is walked
# in it too, so a client accepting several with equal weight gets the first
# one named. A language with no catalogue on disk is not an error -- gettext
# returns the untranslated text, which is English; what is refused at startup
# is the opposite -- a catalogue that cannot be reached because its language
# is not in this list
SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES=en,ru,zh

# The language for someone who asked for none · one of those listed above ·
# everywhere. That is the ordinary case rather than a rare one: neither API
# clients nor the automated tests send Accept-Language at all. Mail sent
# without a request goes in it too -- a task queued before a language reached
# the context. A value outside SUPPORTED_LANGUAGES is refused at startup
DEFAULT_LANGUAGE=en


# ==============================================================================
# 12. For docker compose only
# ==============================================================================
# These variables are read by compose itself; the application never sees them.

# The env file compose hands to the containers · a path · compose.
# The value has to match what is given to --env-file
ENV_FILE=.env.docker

# Which infrastructure services to bring up as our own · a comma-separated
# list of db, cache, broker, mail · compose.
#
#   db      PostgreSQL           otherwise set DATABASE_URL
#   cache   Redis for the cache  otherwise set REDIS_URL
#   broker  Redis for Celery     otherwise set CELERY_BROKER_URL
#   mail    the Mailpit catcher  otherwise set MAIL_HOST and MAIL_PORT
#   logs    journal rotation     otherwise rotate them yourself: without it
#                                the files grow until the disk ends
#
# Empty -- only migrations, app and celery_worker come up. The mail profile
# is declared in dockers/docker-compose.override.yml and has no effect in the
# production form of the stack
COMPOSE_PROFILES=db,cache,broker,mail,logs

# Which compose files make up the stack · paths separated by colons ·
# compose. The files live in dockers/, and without this variable every
# command would need two -f flags. The production form is without the second
# file:
#   docker compose -f dockers/docker-compose.yml --env-file .env.docker up -d
COMPOSE_FILE=dockers/docker-compose.yml:dockers/docker-compose.override.yml

# The source directory mounted into the container for hot reload · a path ·
# compose.
# Compose counts a relative path from its own file's directory, that is from
# dockers/ -- hence the `../`
APP_SRC_PATH=../src

# The directory on the host the journal files are put in · a path · compose
LOG_HOST_PATH=../datas/logs

# How often the rotator looks at the journals, in seconds · a whole number
# > 0 · compose. Not the rotation frequency: a file is moved aside once a
# day, the audit journal once a week. The look is what makes the 100 MB
# ceiling work at all, since that one is only examined at the moment of the
# call. It applies when the logs profile is on
LOG_ROTATE_INTERVAL=3600

# The ceiling docker's driver puts on a container's log · a size and a number
# of files · compose.
# A second stream besides the journal files: gunicorn's own log goes there
# (--access-logfile -) and so does everything celery writes. The json-file
# driver has no bound by default, and without these two values it piles up in
# /var/lib/docker until the disk ends
DOCKER_LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
DOCKER_LOG_MAX_FILE=5

# Where to keep PostgreSQL's data · a path on the host or a volume name ·
# compose. Empty -- the data is in the named volume postgres_data, managed by
# docker. A value with '/' or './' mounts a directory: that is how the
# database can be put on a separate disk and backed up from the host
# DATABASE_DATA_PATH=/srv/link-shortener/postgres

# Where to keep the Redis cache's data · a path on the host or a volume name
# · compose
# REDIS_DATA_PATH=/srv/link-shortener/redis

# Where to keep the Redis broker's data · a path on the host or a volume name
# · compose. The queue survives a restart: tasks queued before it are not
# lost
# REDIS_BROKER_DATA_PATH=/srv/link-shortener/redis-broker

# The number of gunicorn worker processes · a whole number > 0 · compose.
# The number of cores is the guide, and no more than that. The measurement
# table is in docs/development.md, "Load profile"
GUNICORN_WORKERS=4

# The class of the worker process · sync | gthread | gevent · compose.
# Change it with care: GUNICORN_TIMEOUT interrupts a stuck request only on
# sync
GUNICORN_WORKER_CLASS=sync

# The ceiling on one request, in seconds · a whole number > 0 · compose.
# The last line against a hung dependency: a frozen database server does not
# run its own statement_timeout, and the timeouts below it never fire
GUNICORN_TIMEOUT=30

# How many seconds a request is given to finish during a restart · a whole
# number > 0 · compose. It has to be strictly smaller than GUNICORN_TIMEOUT
GUNICORN_GRACEFUL_TIMEOUT=20
